# P1: closing the tile-scaling plateau — the fix, measured

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release profile (lto = "fat", codegen-units = 1), DEFAULT
       features (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16 — the CHECKED `DisjointMut` build,
       tracker armed). No `-C target-cpu=native`.
branch: perf/p1-barrier, off perf/shard-tracker @ 34b0505.
follows: benchmarks/p1_scaling_2026-08-07.meta (the diagnosis this acts on).

## nice level — read this before reproducing

NO `nice` WAS APPLIED TO ANY TIMED RUN. On Darwin a positive nice value maps
the process to background QoS and distorts wall clock by well over an order of
magnitude. `nice -n 19` was used for BUILDS only.

Arms are interleaved back to back within each (vector, threads) cell and the
arm order rotates every round; every number is a MEDIAN OF 9 (3 rounds x 3
reps x 4 iters). `scripts/perf/p1_barrier_sweep.sh` refuses to start, and
discards and re-runs the cell, while any non-agent process is above 25% CPU.

## The arms

Three commits on one branch; each arm is a full build of that commit, so an
arm difference is exactly that commit's diff.

  A  27f5e42  baseline. 32-shard tracker + the `check_tile` deblock barrier +
              the wide CDEF padding guards. Functionally identical to
              perf/shard-tracker @ 34b0505 — the only delta is inert
              `probe-*` cargo features that `default` does not enable.
  B  fdd6a35  A + exact-window CDEF padding guards.
  C  054e2ed  B + the `check_tile` deblock barrier removed.
  E  ba74cc4  C + the loop filter's per-tap borrows replaced by one guard per
              tap row.

## Result 1 — where it lands

v4k_8tile, 3840x2160, 4 tile cols x 2 tile rows, ms/frame, median of 9.

  8bpc                t=1     t=2     t=4     t=8   t1->t8
  A  baseline       605.5   431.7   344.2   332.5    1.82x
  C  no barrier     607.5   373.3   245.9   277.7    2.19x
  E  + loopfilter   546.8   326.7   206.4   200.7    2.72x
  ratio E/A         0.903   0.757   0.600   0.604

  10bpc                             t=4     t=8
  A  baseline                     426.8   423.6
  C  no barrier                   287.7   317.4
  E  + loopfilter                 238.3   234.0
  ratio E/A                       0.558   0.552

* **1.66x at 8 threads, 1.67x at 4, 1.81x at 10bpc t=8**, and 1.11x at t=1 —
  the loop-filter change is a single-thread win too, because the borrows cost
  even when nothing contends them.
* Best shippable configuration goes from 332.5 ms (A at t=8) to 200.7 ms
  (E at t=8): **1.66x**. Against A's best at ANY thread count, same number.
* The t=4 -> t=8 inversion that the barrier fix re-armed (245.9 -> 277.7) is
  gone (206.4 -> 200.7). It is flat, not inverted.
* Every arm, every cell, every thread count: one md5 per vector
  (`a00c11f4...` 8bpc, `840d4364...` 10bpc).

## Result 2 — the diagnosis reproduced, including its warning

The prior recon predicted, from an ablation build, that removing the barrier
ALONE would be worth only ~1.19x at t=8 and would re-introduce anti-scaling
(t=4 244.8 -> t=8 279.7 ms). Arm C, measured independently on the real
shipping build: 1.20x at t=8, t=4 245.9 -> t=8 277.7 ms. Within 0.5% on both
numbers. The "neither term pays alone" finding was correct and this is what
made it worth doing the tracker half in the same pass.

## Result 3 — the barrier was standing in for a 2-byte guard-width bug

`check_tile`'s deblock barrier (06160a6) gated reconstruction of frame-sbrow N
in ANY tile on a frame-global counter advanced by the serial deblock chain.
It cost 2.19x at 8 threads. What it was suppressing:

  cdef_arm.rs:124  &    _[138270..138282]  vs  &mut _[134432..138272]
  cdef_arm.rs:464  &    _[184348..184372]  vs  &mut _[176672..184352]

138272 - 134432 = 3840 = one 4K luma row, so the writer (`backup2lines`
saving a CDEF line-buffer row) holds row R. The reader's guard starts at
138270 = R_end - 2 and the first byte it actually reads is `guard[x_start]`
= 138272, the first byte of row R+1. The two overlapping bytes are guarded
and never dereferenced — the left-padding columns that `x_start = 2` skips
when `HAVE_LEFT` is absent, which at the left frame edge live in the previous
row. Same shape at 10bpc with 7680-byte rows and 4 bytes.

f9458f4 (2026-07-23) diagnosed and fixed exactly this for the scalar
reference in `src/cdef.rs` and stopped there; the SIMD padding functions — all
of them on aarch64, and both bottom-edge loops in the portable/x86 file — never
got it. fdd6a35 propagates the rule and adds it as a module note.

No write hazard justified the barrier either. `rav1d_loopfilter_sbrow_rows(N)`
walks the horizontal edges at 4-row units inside sbrow N; the taps reach at
most 6 rows either side and the filter length is bounded by the transform
heights on both sides, which cannot cross a superblock boundary — so the last
edge of an sbrow is at most a 4-tap. Its writes stay inside sbrow N and the
tail of sbrow N-1; they never reach sbrow N+1, which is all that concurrent
reconstruction touches. The introducing commit had the direction backwards.
dav1d being bit-exact at every thread count with no equivalent rule is the
same statement from the other side.

## Result 4 — the loop filter was 44.6% of the borrow tracker

`sample`, 10 s, v4k_8tile 8bpc t=8, arm C. Every subtree rooted at a tracker
frame (`BorrowTracker`, `TinyLock`, guard drop glue) charged to its nearest
non-tracker caller; 20,800 of 49,137 tracker samples land under a truncated
`<unknown binary>` frame and are excluded, leaving 28,337 attributed:

  src/loopfilter.rs  loop_filter        12,653   44.6%
  picture.rs         WithOffset::…       4,311   15.2%   (inlined index_mut/slice)
  src/itx.rs         inv_txfm_add        1,519    5.4%
  src/ipred.rs       splat_dc              766    2.7%
  src/ipred.rs       cfl_ac_rust           603    2.1%
  src/ipred.rs       cfl_pred              603    2.1%
  src/cdef.rs        cdef_direct           411    1.4%

`loop_filter` took a one-pixel mutable borrow per tap read AND per tap write —
~26 per column, ~104 per call — through
`(dst + strideb * k).index_mut::<BD>()`. `wd` fixes the tap window before any
pixel is touched, and four columns share it, so a call's whole read set is a
rectangle of at most 14x4. ba74cc4 reads that rectangle in with one guard per
picture row, runs the filter on a stack array with no tracking at all, and
writes back only the pixels that changed.

This is a non-x86 problem specifically: `loopfilter_arm::loopfilter_sb_dispatch`
returns `false` (issue #1) and the portable `safe_simd::loopfilter` is x86
intrinsics, so aarch64, wasm and every scalar fallback ran the per-pixel path
while x86_64 already had a compact one.

## Gates

* **Bit identity.** Full `cargo test --release --no-fail-fast` before and
  after: 89 passed / 34 failed both times — the same 34 pre-existing failures
  — and the 1,707 distinct `MISMATCH [tier]: <id> expected=… actual=…` triples
  are byte-identical between the two logs (`diff` is empty). The failure set
  did not grow and no `actual=` hash moved.
* **mt_stress** passes at threads 1/2/4/8/16.
* **Concurrent-process stress**, tracker armed, on the shipped build: 16
  decoders at t=8/12/16/24 across v4k_8tile, v4k_8tile_10b, v4k_1tile and
  v1024 — 0 overlap panics, 0 panics of any kind, one md5 per vector. Plus 20
  repeats of the two configurations that failed during development.
* **Detector liveness.** Not assumed: the same stress on the barrier-ablated
  build with the WIDE CDEF guards panics 2 of 5 processes with
  `overlapping DisjointMut` at cdef_arm.rs:124/464, and 0 of 16 with the
  narrow guards. The detector fires and stops firing on the same code path
  with a one-line change, so the clean runs above are evidence, not silence.
  See "Liveness B" below for the loop-filter path.
* `forbid(unsafe_code)` holds; no `unsafe` was added anywhere.
* clippy `--release --all-targets` clean on the touched files.

### Liveness B — the loop-filter path, and an honest limit on the probe

The detector demonstrably fires at the two call sites `LfBlock` uses. The
run-level variant of this same code (see "tried and measured worse" below) —
identical `slice::<BD>(w)` read at picture.rs:697 and `slice_mut` write-back at
picture.rs:710, only with the rectangle widened to `4 * steps` columns —
panicked in 4 of 16 development runs:

  picture.rs:697   &    _[3951104..3951296]  vs  &mut _[3951216..3951224]
  picture.rs:710   &mut _[12293056..12293072] vs    & _[12292864..12293104]

Two deliberately-widened variants of the SHIPPED code did NOT reproduce within
the 16-process stress, and this is reported as a limit of the probe rather than
as a clean bill of health:

  * write-back widened from the changed span to the whole row: 0 panics
  * read guard widened by 32 pixels beyond what is copied: 0 panics

The probability is evidently steep in the width — 128 extra columns trips it,
32 does not, within this stress budget — so these two nulls do not show the
outermost taps are safe to guard. They show the probe is not sensitive enough
to settle it. The shipped code keeps both narrowings on the argument that
holds without a stress result: the rectangle is bounded by the direct path's
own read set for the same `wd`, and the write-back is bounded by what changed.

## What was tried and measured WORSE or NULL

* **`TinyLock` spin backoff** (yield to the scheduler after 64 spins).
  The prior recon flagged the bare `AtomicBool` spin as the wrong primitive
  once real concurrency arrives, and its `sample` share does rise to 22.9%
  with the barrier out. Measured on arm C, median of 9, interleaved:
  t=8 276.8 -> 274.7 (0.992x), t=4 245.9 -> 246.3 (1.001x), 10bpc t=8
  317.3 -> 316.2 (0.996x). Inside noise. **Backed out.** The spin share is a
  symptom of borrow VOLUME, not of the primitive; the loop-filter change
  attacks the volume and is worth 1.38x on the same arm.
* **Run-level (not call-level) compaction of the loop filter's rectangle** —
  one compact copy per `loop_filter_sb128_rust` call instead of per edge,
  which is what the x86 dispatch does. It is faster (t=1 523 ms vs 547) but
  **unsound as written**: the rectangle spans `4 * steps` columns, up to 128
  pixels, including columns for steps the run skips, and reading them
  collides with a concurrent writer. Reproduced as a real overlap panic
  (`& _[3951104..3951296]` vs `&mut _[3951216..3951224]`, 3 of 8 runs at
  10bpc t=4). The per-call rectangle is bounded by the direct path's own read
  set for that `wd`, which is what makes it safe. Do not "optimise" it back.

## Where this leaves the gap

Against dav1d 1.5.4 `--framedelay 1` (tile threading only — the same threading
model), measured here, not quoted: same host, same session, wall clock with a
two-point (2 vs 20 frame) fit that removes process startup, median of 3,
interleaved against our binaries with the same idle guard.
`scripts/perf/p1_dav1d_wall.sh`, raw in `p1_barrier_dav1d_2026-08-07.tsv`.

  ms/frame                 A        E    dav1d      E/dav1d    A/dav1d
  v4k_8tile      t=1   604.7    547.5    246.6         2.22       2.45
  v4k_8tile      t=8   333.6    200.2     34.3         5.83       9.72
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=1   725.8    630.9    251.1         2.51       2.89
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=8   421.7    233.6     35.7         6.55      11.82

  t1 -> t8 scaling       A 1.81x    E 2.74x    dav1d 7.18x

**The 8-thread gap goes 9.72x -> 5.83x (8bpc) and 11.82x -> 6.55x (10bpc);
single-thread 2.45x -> 2.22x.** This instrument reproduces the interleaved
sweep above to within 0.3% on all four rav1d-safe cells (604.7 vs 605.5,
333.6 vs 332.5, 547.5 vs 546.8, 200.2 vs 200.7) and the earlier committed
dav1d yardstick to within 0.3%, so the two measurements are consistent.

The remaining constraint at 8 threads is the post-filter chain, which the
diagnosis measured at 134.5 ms/frame of CPU running ~2 wide, and which is a
P2 kernel problem: `src/safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs`, `loopfilter_arm.rs` and
`looprestoration_arm.rs` contain ZERO aarch64 intrinsics — the "NEON" tier for
those three is the scalar reference (`ROADMAP_SIMD_PORTING.md` row R3, still
TODO; sibling record `benchmarks/p2_kernel_profile_2026-08-07.meta` puts CDEF
at 17.3x and the loop filter at 11.3x dav1d at t=1). Nothing in this pass
touched a kernel's arithmetic.

## Data files

  p1_barrier_2026-08-07.tsv         the A/B/C sweep, 108 rows
  p1_barrier_ace_2026-08-07.tsv     the A/C/E sweep, 162 rows
  p1_barrier_lock_ab_2026-08-07.tsv the rejected TinyLock backoff, 54 rows
  p1_barrier_dav1d_2026-08-07.tsv   dav1d vs A vs E, wall-clock two-point fit
  (columns: round, vector, threads, arm, ms_per_frame, md5)

## Reproducing

  # one target dir per arm; nice is for BUILDS only
  for c in 27f5e42:A fdd6a35:B 054e2ed:C ba74cc4:E; do
    git archive ${c%%:*} | tar -x -C ~/tmp/rav1d-p1fix/src-${c##*:}
    CARGO_TARGET_DIR=~/tmp/rav1d-p1fix/tgt-${c##*:} RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache \
      nice -n 19 cargo build --release --example bench_ab_decode -j 4
  done
  ARMS="A C E" ./scripts/perf/p1_barrier_sweep.sh out.tsv 3 3 4
